data: {"type":"response.reasoning_summary_text.done","summary_index":0,"item_id":"rs_02179466137197200000000000000000000ffffac190324999ea9","output_index":0,"text":"We need answer only JSON object with declaration, implementation, supporting_objects. Need use tools compile/simulate/verify max 3 each. Need save exact final source to candidate.json — but filesystem read-only! Sandbox read-only. Hmm. We can perhaps not write files? The instruction says save candidate.json. But permissions read-only, no escalation allowed. Could we use tools only. Shell commands unnecessary. We may not be able to save. But primary task: return JSON only. We can attempt writing candidate.json? Read-only likely fails. Maybe we should just return JSON. But instruction says save exact final submitted source to candidate.json. Need obey if possible. The MCP tools maybe compile/simulate. We can use them. We should craft ST FB.\n\nLet's inspect nothing? \"Do not inspect other directories...\" We can reason.\n\nNeed design FB_Task with exact vars, DUTs referenced. Interface only inputs/outputs. Internal vars allowed. Need ST body.\n\nLet's understand behavior thoroughly and formal predicates.\n\nSystem:\n- 5 stations 1..5.\n- IDLE. sensor1 true and station1Start rising edge => start: query processSequence[1], target, move direction, if target maybe same as current? Active sequence final entry 5. Processing stations 2-4; active entries maybe repeat consecutively. At start from station1, target first entry could be 2,3,4, or 5. They say system starts conveyor to corresponding workstation and stops after delivery.\n- Upon processing completion at corresponding station, continues to next element. Determine direction and start. If next station same as current? Assumptions: \"Consecutive equal stations denote separate processing operations with fresh edges and no conveyor movement.\" So after completion, if next target equals current, immediately enter WAIT_PROCESS (no movement). Need perhaps accept completion edge only at current processing wait, fresh.\n- Final active entry output station 5. Arrival at 5 => idle.\n\nNeed handle sensor arrival at target. Upon arrival stop motor and enter WAIT_PROCESS (if processing station 2-4), or idle if target 5. The outputs observed post-scan must be stop. The target sensor sampling.\n\nNeed direction:\n- higher: post-phase MOVING AND targetStation>currentStation AND NOT sensor[targetStation] -> right true left false.\n- lower similarly.\n- arrive pre-phase MOVING sensor target -> stop.\nAtomic \"higher\"/\"lower\" post-phase MOVING meaning outputs correspond at observation. So when moving toward higher target, right on. If target same current and we enter WAIT_PROCESS, no move. Good.\n\nNeed start rising edge. station1Start_rejected pre-phase IDLE AND station1Start AND NOT sensor1 => stop. Could simply only accept when sensor1 true and rising edge. If station1Start true w/o edge and sensor1 false, stop. IDLE no conveyor anyway.\n\nNeed \"station1Start is a rising edge accepted with sensor1 while idle.\" So detect rising edge of station1Start. Internal `startPrev` track. Start when IDLE, sensor1, station1Start AND NOT startPrev.\n\nPotential issue processLength 0/out of range? Contract 1..8 with final 5. We can assume valid. But robust. Need sequenceIndex. Initial sequenceIndex 0, target 1 current 1. On start, sequenceIndex := 1? initial says sequenceIndex 0. Query first. We can do `sequenceIndex := 1; targetStation := processSequence[1]` and determine if target=current? Start from station1, target could 1? Contract says other active entries 2-4, final 5, so first likely processing or 5, not 1. Fine.\n\nOn destination arrival:\n- If target=5: currentStation:=5; targetStation:=5? phase idle; sequenceIndex maybe processLength; outputs stopped.\n- Else target 2..4: currentStation:=target; phase := WAIT_PROCESS; sequenceIndex remains index. So completion can be accepted.\n- Completion edges: At WAIT_PROCESS, matching station complete rising edge. Need track previous complete signals. Fresh edge. If currentStation=2 and station2Complete AND NOT prevComplete2. On fresh edge:\n  - If sequenceIndex < processLength then sequenceIndex++ ; nextStation := processSequence[sequenceIndex]. current station is currentStation (2..4). If nextStation = currentStation => stay WAIT_PROCESS? This represents another processing operation requiring fresh edge. But \"Consecutive equal stations denote separate processing operations with fresh edges and no conveyor movement.\" Upon consuming a completion for station 2, if next target 2, enter WAIT_PROCESS at same station and wait for fresh completion edge. Since station2Complete remains true, need fresh edge: it must drop then rise. We can just remain WAIT_PROCESS and rely on edge detector. Good.\n  - If next target > currentStation => MOVING, targetStation := next, conveyorRight true, left false.\n  - If next < currentStation => MOVING, left true.\n  - If next=5 and current maybe 4 -> right, or current maybe 3 -> right. eventually.\n  - Outputs after processing transition: \"Upon receiving processing completion signal ... continues to query next element, determines direction, and starts conveyor.\" So on completion scan, outputs can start in same transition. Yes.\n  - What if next = 5 and current = 5? But completion only at current 2-4, final 5 no wait. So no.\n- When moving, at beginning of scan check target sensor. If active -> stop and transition. Arrival immediate stop. If target 5 idle. If target station 2..4 WAIT_PROCESS. For processing station, probably sensor stays true. Then wait. Good.\n- During MOVING if sensor target already true same scan as start? Could start from IDLE with sensor target already true. E.g., processSequence[1]=2 and sensor2 already true at start. If we set MOVING and not check arrival until next scan, then post-phase MOVING target 2 > current 1 and sensor2 true, so predicate `higher` = false because NOT sensor[targetStation] false, arrive pre-phase? arrive uses pre-phase MOVING, false during start scan, so no requirement. But physical product at target; should stop immediately perhaps. Requirement \"starts conveyor to move raw material to corresponding workstation, and stops after delivery.\" If sensor already true before starting, likely should stop same scan. Need maybe transition directly to WAIT_PROCESS if target sensor already true at start. Better. But are inputs coherent? They may set sensor target only after movement. Yet formal verification may include arbitrary inputs and check. Could handle.\n\n- Conveyor outputs should never both true.\n- P7 if IDLE and station1Start pressed with sensor1 false: stop. IDLE always stop.\n- Direction P2 formal requires in MOVING post-phase and target > current and not sensor target, right true. Need set conveyor outputs based on target/current every scan. If in MOVING and destination not reached. We can at start pre-arrival check. Then outputs on. If arrival, phase changes WAIT_PROCESS/IDLE and outputs stop. So post-phase MOVING only when no target sensor. Good. Direction uses post-phase MOVING. If target same? phase not MOVING. Good.\n\nNeed formal verification tool: compile_st then verify_st. `verify_st` with source. We need craft declaration/implementation/supporting_objects. Could define enum DUT for phase? Maybe easier use internal INT state constants. Supporting objects maybe DUT `E_ProcessPhase` enumeration. But formal verifier may handle. Requirement says \"Define referenced DUTs.\" If we reference no DUT, supporting_objects empty is okay. But declaration uses ARRAY[1..8]. No DUT needed. Maybe keep supporting_objects []. Simpler is safest.\n\nNeed CODESYS 3.5 ST syntax. Function block:\n\nFUNCTION_BLOCK FB_Task\nVAR_INPUT\n  processLength : USINT;\n  sensor1 : BOOL;\n...\n  processSequence : ARRAY[1..8] OF USINT;\nEND_VAR\nVAR_OUTPUT\n  conveyorLeft : BOOL;\n  conveyorRight : BOOL;\nEND_VAR\nVAR\n  phase : INT := 0; // IDLE 0, MOVING 1, WAIT_PROCESS 2\n  currentStation : USINT := 1;\n  targetStation : USINT := 1;\n  sequenceIndex : USINT := 0;\n  startPrev : BOOL;\n  complete2Prev : BOOL;\n  complete3Prev : BOOL;\n  complete4Prev : BOOL;\n  nextStation : USINT;\n  freshComplete : BOOL;\nEND_VAR\n\nImplementation body:\n```\n// defaults\nconveyorLeft := FALSE;\nconveyorRight := FALSE;\nfreshComplete := FALSE;\n\nCASE phase OF\n0: // IDLE\n    IF sensor1 AND station1Start AND NOT startPrev THEN\n        sequenceIndex := 1;\n        targetStation := processSequence[1];\n        IF targetStation = 1 THEN\n            // weird; maybe target 1. Since final 5 etc. Could...\n        END_IF\n        IF targetStation = currentStation THEN ...\n```\nNeed careful priority when phase transitions and same-scan outputs. We can't CASE and then recompute outputs afterwards perhaps easier use state machine as IFs and final `IF phase = 1 THEN outputs based on target-curr`. But when transition occurs in scan, `phase` new maybe WAIT_PROCESS/idle so outputs false. Good. For transitions from completion to MOVING, phase becomes 1 and final block sets direction. Nice. For start, if target not already sensor, phase=1; final outputs set. If target sensor true, target>=2 -> WAIT_PROCESS or idle. Great.\n\nCould centralize arrival detection before completion? Need priority. In MOVING phase, arrival at target takes priority and stops; processing completion should be ignored while moving. If at WAIT_PROCESS, arrival no relevance. Movement only while phase=MOVING.\n\nPseudo:\n\n```\nconveyorLeft := FALSE;\nconveyorRight := FALSE;\nfreshComplete := FALSE;\n\nCASE phase OF\n0: (* IDLE *)\n  IF startPrev = FALSE AND station1Start AND sensor1 THEN\n    sequenceIndex := 1;\n    targetStation := processSequence[sequenceIndex];\n    IF targetStation = 1 THEN\n      currentStation := 1;\n      phase := 0;\n    ELSIF targetStation = 5 THEN\n      (* perhaps if sensor5 already true? *)\n      IF sensor5 THEN\n         currentStation :=5;\n         sequenceIndex := processLength;\n         phase := 0;\n      ELSE\n         phase := 1;\n      END_IF\n    ELSE\n      IF StationSensor(targetStation) THEN\n         currentStation := targetStation;\n         phase := 2;\n      ELSE\n         phase := 1;\n      END_IF\n    END_IF\n  END_IF\n1: (* MOVING *)\n  IF TargetSensor(targetStation) THEN\n     currentStation := targetStation;\n     IF targetStation = 5 THEN\n        sequenceIndex := processLength;\n        phase := 0;\n     ELSE\n        phase := 2;\n     END_IF\n  END_IF\n2: (* WAIT_PROCESS *)\n  freshComplete := (currentStation = 2 AND station2Complete AND NOT complete2Prev) OR ...\n  IF freshComplete AND sequenceIndex < processLength THEN\n     sequenceIndex := sequenceIndex + 1;\n     targetStation := processSequence[sequenceIndex];\n     IF targetStation = currentStation THEN\n        (* next processing at same station: remain wait, fresh edge consumed by prev tracking *)\n        phase := 2;\n     ELSIF targetStation = 5 THEN\n        IF sensor5 THEN\n           currentStation :=5; sequenceIndex:=processLength; phase:=0;\n        ELSE\n           phase:=1;\n        END_IF\n     ELSE\n        IF targetStation<currentStation OR targetStation>currentStation? etc\n           phase:=1, maybe direct wait if target sensor=\n        END_IF\n     END_IF\n  END_IF\nEND_CASE\n\nIF phase = 1 THEN\n  IF targetStation > currentStation THEN conveyorRight := TRUE;\n  ELSIF targetStation < currentStation THEN conveyorLeft := TRUE;\n  END_IF\nEND_IF\n\nstartPrev := station1Start;\ncomplete2Prev := station2Complete;\n...\n```\nPotential issue direct target sensor check in WAIT transition. Suppose next target is station3 and sensor3 true from current? Product is at station2, sensor3 shouldn't be true, unless stale. Contract. Could set phase MOVING and next scan stop. But P2 post-phase MOVING with sensor target true gives no requirement. Next scan arrival stops. Still safe but unnecessary one scan motor on despite already at target. Better direct check. But if sensor target true due to stale, should stop. Use helper logic for \"beginTravelTo\". Can't call functions unless define. Inline repeated.\n\nNeed no new DUT. But we need check sensors by target. Could use CASE. To check target sensor:\n```\nIF ((targetStation=2) AND sensor2) OR ... \n```\nCould define internal `targetPresent : BOOL`.\n\nIn IDLE start:\n```\ntargetPresent := (targetStation = 2 AND sensor2) OR ... \n```\nNeed avoid short-circuit? CODESYS supports AND OR.\n\nFor target=5 if sensor5 true then done immediate. But start requirement? Product raw at station1, sensor5 false. Fine.\n\nFor target=1? Contract says other active entries 2..4 final 5; first cannot 1. Could still implement gracefully: if target=1 then current=1 wait? Station1 has no completion. Better idle? Don't need. But ensure no division. We can treat as waiting at station1? Not valid. Maybe simply if targetStation = 1 then currentStation:=1; phase:=0 (no movement). This preserves stop. Not relevant.\n\nNeed what happens if processLength=1 and processSequence[1]=5: start and move to 5. Good.\n\nNeed currentStation updated on arrival. In MOVING target. If target 5 current 5. good.\n\nNeed phase initial values: internal vars default initialization in declaration. CODESYS initializes to specified. Fine.\n\nNeed station1Start rising edge accepted \"rising edge\" could mean if start already true on first scan with sensor1, accepted? Edge relative to initial false, yes startPrev initialized FALSE, so accepted. Good.\n\nNeed completions rising edge detection. completePrev initialized false means if station2Complete already true at first wait scan, considered fresh edge. Is that right? Since fresh edge relative to instance previous scan. If station2Complete was true while moving, the previous scan should have been tracked true. We update prev each scan regardless phase. If it rose while moving and remains true when entering wait, by wait scan prev is true -> no fresh. Good. If station2Complete already true from initial and not processed? The assumptions likely no. Initial complete signals are false? Not specified but likely. If previous scan false at start and remains. fine.\n\nNeed arrivals and processing completion priorities. Suppose pre-phase MOVING and target sensor true (arrive). Even if completion signal high, we transition WAIT_PROCESS; we should not process complete in same scan because completion ignored unless post-entry wait. Our CASE phase=1 branch only arrival. Good. `freshComplete` computed only in phase 2, so false. Next scan at wait, prev complete tracked. If completion high during arrival scan, prev set true so no fresh next scan. Assumption says \"Station completion edges are ignored unless post-entry phase is WAIT_PROCESS\" yes.\n\nSuppose waiting and completion fresh: process. If next target same as current, remain WAIT_PROCESS. But consuming fresh edge by prev update end scan. Next scan still high -> no edge. When drops then rises -> fresh. Good.\n\nPotential P6 conditional completion: If moving -> F arrive; processing -> F completion; started -> F done. Our FB waiting can proceed with completions. For consecutive equal stations, success. Good.\n\nBut formal verifier may require `done` eventually under valid process sequence. Our state machine should.\n\nNeed think about simultaneous arrival and next? Only arrival, wait next scan completion needed fresh. Good.\n\nCould use USINT sequenceIndex increments to 8 max, okay. processLength valid.\n\nNeed maybe no direct sensor check same scan to align LTL P2 `higher` uses post-phase MOVING and `NOT sensor[target]`. If targetPresent false we set phase moving. Fine. If target present, phase wait/idle. `higher` false. Good.\n\nNow compile and verify. We need provide candidate to compile_st. Let's use exact source. But before tools, do we need inspect? No. Let's draft.\n\nNeed decide support object for helper? None. `supporting_objects`: [].\n\nCould we use local helper method? No.\n\nPotential syntax of CASE: CODESYS `CASE phase OF` with integer labels `0:` and `1:`. Stops at END_CASE. Fine.\n\nVariable names might collide? `targetStation`, `currentStation`, internal. Interface exact.\n\nPotential issue `sequenceIndex` initial 0 but processSequence[1]. Fine.\n\nLet's write implementation concise:\n\n```\nconveyorLeft := FALSE;\nconveyorRight := FALSE;\n\ntargetPresent := FALSE;\nCASE targetStation OF\n  2: targetPresent := sensor2;\n  3: targetPresent := sensor3;\n  4: targetPresent := sensor4;\n  5: targetPresent := sensor5;\nELSE\n  targetPresent := FALSE;\nEND_CASE;\n\nCASE phase OF\n0:\n  IF station1Start AND NOT startPrev AND sensor1 THEN\n    sequenceIndex := 1;\n    targetStation := processSequence[1];\n    currentStation := 1;\n    IF targetStation = 1 THEN\n      phase := 0;\n    ELSIF targetPresent THEN\n      currentStation := targetStation;\n      IF targetStation = 5 THEN\n        sequenceIndex := processLength;\n        phase := 0;\n      ELSE\n        phase := 2;\n      END_IF\n    ELSE\n      phase := 1;\n    END_IF\n  END_IF\n1:\n  IF targetPresent THEN\n    currentStation := targetStation;\n    IF targetStation = 5 THEN\n      sequenceIndex := processLength;\n      phase := 0;\n    ELSE\n      phase := 2;\n    END_IF\n  END_IF\n2:\n  freshComplete := ((currentStation = 2) AND station2Complete AND NOT complete2Prev)\n               OR ((currentStation = 3) AND station3Complete AND NOT complete3Prev)\n               OR ((currentStation = 4) AND station4Complete AND NOT complete4Prev);\n\n  IF freshComplete AND (sequenceIndex < processLength) THEN\n    sequenceIndex := sequenceIndex + 1;\n    targetStation := processSequence[sequenceIndex];\n\n    (* recompute target presence for new target *)\n    targetPresent := FALSE;\n    CASE targetStation OF\n      2: targetPresent := sensor2;\n      ...\n    END_CASE\n\n    IF targetStation = currentStation THEN\n       phase := 2;\n    ELSIF targetPresent THEN\n       currentStation := targetStation;\n       IF targetStation = 5 THEN\n          sequenceIndex := processLength;\n          phase := 0;\n       ELSE\n          phase := 2;\n       END_IF\n    ELSE\n       phase := 1;\n    END_IF\n  END_IF\nEND_CASE\n\nIF phase = 1 THEN\n  IF targetStation > currentStation THEN\n    conveyorRight := TRUE;\n    conveyorLeft := FALSE;\n  ELSIF targetStation < currentStation THEN\n    conveyorLeft := TRUE;\n    conveyorRight := FALSE;\n  END_IF\nEND_IF\n\nstartPrev := station1Start;\ncomplete2Prev := station2Complete;\ncomplete3Prev := station3Complete;\ncomplete4Prev := station4Complete;\n```\n\nConcern: In WAIT_PROCESS, `targetStation` is current station. If `targetStation=5` never wait. If currentStation 2/3/4. Good.\nConcern: processLength maybe 0, sequenceIndex< processLength false. no.\nConcern: sequenceIndex can equal 8 and increment only if < processLength, max 8. `processLength` USINT. Fine.\nConcern: if next target 5 and targetPresent true (sensor5 high), transition idle same scan. P5 `out` pre-phase MOVING and targetStation=5 and sensor5 -> done & stop. But this direct transition from WAIT_PROCESS where targetStation was current station then set to 5 and sensor5 true: pre-phase MOVING false, so `out` false. Fine.\nConcern: target 5 arrival in MOVING: we set sequenceIndex := processLength. If current sequenceIndex was processLength (it should be final), okay. This makes index final. Good.\nConcern: if processSequence contains 1? Contract no. Fine.\n\nBut formal verification may use declarations with `VAR` internal? likely. `phase` perhaps verifier expects initial_conditions? The task atomic propositions reference phase names. It may inspect candidate declarations for phase? The spec likely can use state var? \"over declared port names\" for verify_st optional assertions. Hmm. It may compile to SMV and use port values and maybe internal state? The LTL formulas use predicates higher etc based on \"post-phase MOVING\", target/current. Their normalizer likely observes outputs and inputs; perhaps state is inferred? But we cannot name phase for tool. It might not need. Our implementation should.\n\nNeed maybe include `phase` variable name as INT to match initial? It says initial_conditions phase IDLE. Reference doesn't demand exposed. But formal mapping could look for `phase`. Usually tools inspect candidate's internal vars? The tool compile_st/verify_st takes source, likely verifier translates declared state? It may require exactly FB_Task and interface. Internal vars can be anything. But predicates `higher` etc. may rely on generated monitor state? They might symbolically compile ST and map outputs, inputs. Hard to know. Could define internal phase as enum with names IDLE etc. But not necessary. However \"initial_conditions\": phase \"IDLE\", currentStation 1, sequenceIndex 0, target 1. Maybe formal verifier parses internal vars by names? If so using `phase : INT := 0` not matching string \"IDLE\"? They may normalize. Yet likely their harness checks behavior, not exact internal var names, as interface definition only inputs outputs. Atomic propositions mention \"post-phase MOVING\" but that's derived from their monitor expected model, not our code. We need implement.\n\nLet's use compile_st. Need format tool source fields. declaration no END_FUNCTION_BLOCK. implementation body. supporting_objects [].\n\nNeed ensure declaration begins `FUNCTION_BLOCK FB_Task`. We'll call compile.","sequence_number":1605}

event: response.reasoning_summary_part.done
data: {"type":"response.reasoning_summary_part.done","item_id":"rs_02179466137197200000000000000000000ffffac190324999ea9","output_index":0,"summary_index":0,"part":{"type":"summary_text","text":"We need answer only JSON object with declaration, implementation, supporting_objects. Need use tools compile/simulate/verify max 3 each. Need save exact final source to candidate.json — but filesystem read-only! Sandbox read-only. Hmm. We can perhaps not write files? The instruction says save candidate.json. But permissions read-only, no escalation allowed. Could we use tools only. Shell commands unnecessary. We may not be able to save. But primary task: return JSON only. We can attempt writing candidate.json? Read-only likely fails. Maybe we should just return JSON. But instruction says save exact final submitted source to candidate.json. Need obey if possible. The MCP tools maybe compile/simulate. We can use them. We should craft ST FB.\n\nLet's inspect nothing? \"Do not inspect other directories...\" We can reason.\n\nNeed design FB_Task with exact vars, DUTs referenced. Interface only inputs/outputs. Internal vars allowed. Need ST body.\n\nLet's understand behavior thoroughly and formal predicates.\n\nSystem:\n- 5 stations 1..5.\n- IDLE. sensor1 true and station1Start rising edge => start: query processSequence[1], target, move direction, if target maybe same as current? Active sequence final entry 5. Processing stations 2-4; active entries maybe repeat consecutively. At start from station1, target first entry could be 2,3,4, or 5. They say system starts conveyor to corresponding workstation and stops after delivery.\n- Upon processing completion at corresponding station, continues to next element. Determine direction and start. If next station same as current? Assumptions: \"Consecutive equal stations denote separate processing operations with fresh edges and no conveyor movement.\" So after completion, if next target equals current, immediately enter WAIT_PROCESS (no movement). Need perhaps accept completion edge only at current processing wait, fresh.\n- Final active entry output station 5. Arrival at 5 => idle.\n\nNeed handle sensor arrival at target. Upon arrival stop motor and enter WAIT_PROCESS (if processing station 2-4), or idle if target 5. The outputs observed post-scan must be stop. The target sensor sampling.\n\nNeed direction:\n- higher: post-phase MOVING AND targetStation>currentStation AND NOT sensor[targetStation] -> right true left false.\n- lower similarly.\n- arrive pre-phase MOVING sensor target -> stop.\nAtomic \"higher\"/\"lower\" post-phase MOVING meaning outputs correspond at observation. So when moving toward higher target, right on. If target same current and we enter WAIT_PROCESS, no move. Good.\n\nNeed start rising edge. station1Start_rejected pre-phase IDLE AND station1Start AND NOT sensor1 => stop. Could simply only accept when sensor1 true and rising edge. If station1Start true w/o edge and sensor1 false, stop. IDLE no conveyor anyway.\n\nNeed \"station1Start is a rising edge accepted with sensor1 while idle.\" So detect rising edge of station1Start. Internal `startPrev` track. Start when IDLE, sensor1, station1Start AND NOT startPrev.\n\nPotential issue processLength 0/out of range? Contract 1..8 with final 5. We can assume valid. But robust. Need sequenceIndex. Initial sequenceIndex 0, target 1 current 1. On start, sequenceIndex := 1? initial says sequenceIndex 0. Query first. We can do `sequenceIndex := 1; targetStation := processSequence[1]` and determine if target=current? Start from station1, target could 1? Contract says other active entries 2-4, final 5, so first likely processing or 5, not 1. Fine.\n\nOn destination arrival:\n- If target=5: currentStation:=5; targetStation:=5? phase idle; sequenceIndex maybe processLength; outputs stopped.\n- Else target 2..4: currentStation:=target; phase := WAIT_PROCESS; sequenceIndex remains index. So completion can be accepted.\n- Completion edges: At WAIT_PROCESS, matching station complete rising edge. Need track previous complete signals. Fresh edge. If currentStation=2 and station2Complete AND NOT prevComplete2. On fresh edge:\n  - If sequenceIndex < processLength then sequenceIndex++ ; nextStation := processSequence[sequenceIndex]. current station is currentStation (2..4). If nextStation = currentStation => stay WAIT_PROCESS? This represents another processing operation requiring fresh edge. But \"Consecutive equal stations denote separate processing operations with fresh edges and no conveyor movement.\" Upon consuming a completion for station 2, if next target 2, enter WAIT_PROCESS at same station and wait for fresh completion edge. Since station2Complete remains true, need fresh edge: it must drop then rise. We can just remain WAIT_PROCESS and rely on edge detector. Good.\n  - If next target > currentStation => MOVING, targetStation := next, conveyorRight true, left false.\n  - If next < currentStation => MOVING, left true.\n  - If next=5 and current maybe 4 -> right, or current maybe 3 -> right. eventually.\n  - Outputs after processing transition: \"Upon receiving processing completion signal ... continues to query next element, determines direction, and starts conveyor.\" So on completion scan, outputs can start in same transition. Yes.\n  - What if next = 5 and current = 5? But completion only at current 2-4, final 5 no wait. So no.\n- When moving, at beginning of scan check target sensor. If active -> stop and transition. Arrival immediate stop. If target 5 idle. If target station 2..4 WAIT_PROCESS. For processing station, probably sensor stays true. Then wait. Good.\n- During MOVING if sensor target already true same scan as start? Could start from IDLE with sensor target already true. E.g., processSequence[1]=2 and sensor2 already true at start. If we set MOVING and not check arrival until next scan, then post-phase MOVING target 2 > current 1 and sensor2 true, so predicate `higher` = false because NOT sensor[targetStation] false, arrive pre-phase? arrive uses pre-phase MOVING, false during start scan, so no requirement. But physical product at target; should stop immediately perhaps. Requirement \"starts conveyor to move raw material to corresponding workstation, and stops after delivery.\" If sensor already true before starting, likely should stop same scan. Need maybe transition directly to WAIT_PROCESS if target sensor already true at start. Better. But are inputs coherent? They may set sensor target only after movement. Yet formal verification may include arbitrary inputs and check. Could handle.\n\n- Conveyor outputs should never both true.\n- P7 if IDLE and station1Start pressed with sensor1 false: stop. IDLE always stop.\n- Direction P2 formal requires in MOVING post-phase and target > current and not sensor target, right true. Need set conveyor outputs based on target/current every scan. If in MOVING and destination not reached. We can at start pre-arrival check. Then outputs on. If arrival, phase changes WAIT_PROCESS/IDLE and outputs stop. So post-phase MOVING only when no target sensor. Good. Direction uses post-phase MOVING. If target same? phase not MOVING. Good.\n\nNeed formal verification tool: compile_st then verify_st. `verify_st` with source. We need craft declaration/implementation/supporting_objects. Could define enum DUT for phase? Maybe easier use internal INT state constants. Supporting objects maybe DUT `E_ProcessPhase` enumeration. But formal verifier may handle. Requirement says \"Define referenced DUTs.\" If we reference no DUT, supporting_objects empty is okay. But declaration uses ARRAY[1..8]. No DUT needed. Maybe keep supporting_objects []. Simpler is safest.\n\nNeed CODESYS 3.5 ST syntax. Function block:\n\nFUNCTION_BLOCK FB_Task\nVAR_INPUT\n  processLength : USINT;\n  sensor1 : BOOL;\n...\n  processSequence : ARRAY[1..8] OF USINT;\nEND_VAR\nVAR_OUTPUT\n  conveyorLeft : BOOL;\n  conveyorRight : BOOL;\nEND_VAR\nVAR\n  phase : INT := 0; // IDLE 0, MOVING 1, WAIT_PROCESS 2\n  currentStation : USINT := 1;\n  targetStation : USINT := 1;\n  sequenceIndex : USINT := 0;\n  startPrev : BOOL;\n  complete2Prev : BOOL;\n  complete3Prev : BOOL;\n  complete4Prev : BOOL;\n  nextStation : USINT;\n  freshComplete : BOOL;\nEND_VAR\n\nImplementation body:\n```\n// defaults\nconveyorLeft := FALSE;\nconveyorRight := FALSE;\nfreshComplete := FALSE;\n\nCASE phase OF\n0: // IDLE\n    IF sensor1 AND station1Start AND NOT startPrev THEN\n        sequenceIndex := 1;\n        targetStation := processSequence[1];\n        IF targetStation = 1 THEN\n            // weird; maybe target 1. Since final 5 etc. Could...\n        END_IF\n        IF targetStation = currentStation THEN ...\n```\nNeed careful priority when phase transitions and same-scan outputs. We can't CASE and then recompute outputs afterwards perhaps easier use state machine as IFs and final `IF phase = 1 THEN outputs based on target-curr`. But when transition occurs in scan, `phase` new maybe WAIT_PROCESS/idle so outputs false. Good. For transitions from completion to MOVING, phase becomes 1 and final block sets direction. Nice. For start, if target not already sensor, phase=1; final outputs set. If target sensor true, target>=2 -> WAIT_PROCESS or idle. Great.\n\nCould centralize arrival detection before completion? Need priority. In MOVING phase, arrival at target takes priority and stops; processing completion should be ignored while moving. If at WAIT_PROCESS, arrival no relevance. Movement only while phase=MOVING.\n\nPseudo:\n\n```\nconveyorLeft := FALSE;\nconveyorRight := FALSE;\nfreshComplete := FALSE;\n\nCASE phase OF\n0: (* IDLE *)\n  IF startPrev = FALSE AND station1Start AND sensor1 THEN\n    sequenceIndex := 1;\n    targetStation := processSequence[sequenceIndex];\n    IF targetStation = 1 THEN\n      currentStation := 1;\n      phase := 0;\n    ELSIF targetStation = 5 THEN\n      (* perhaps if sensor5 already true? *)\n      IF sensor5 THEN\n         currentStation :=5;\n         sequenceIndex := processLength;\n         phase := 0;\n      ELSE\n         phase := 1;\n      END_IF\n    ELSE\n      IF StationSensor(targetStation) THEN\n         currentStation := targetStation;\n         phase := 2;\n      ELSE\n         phase := 1;\n      END_IF\n    END_IF\n  END_IF\n1: (* MOVING *)\n  IF TargetSensor(targetStation) THEN\n     currentStation := targetStation;\n     IF targetStation = 5 THEN\n        sequenceIndex := processLength;\n        phase := 0;\n     ELSE\n        phase := 2;\n     END_IF\n  END_IF\n2: (* WAIT_PROCESS *)\n  freshComplete := (currentStation = 2 AND station2Complete AND NOT complete2Prev) OR ...\n  IF freshComplete AND sequenceIndex < processLength THEN\n     sequenceIndex := sequenceIndex + 1;\n     targetStation := processSequence[sequenceIndex];\n     IF targetStation = currentStation THEN\n        (* next processing at same station: remain wait, fresh edge consumed by prev tracking *)\n        phase := 2;\n     ELSIF targetStation = 5 THEN\n        IF sensor5 THEN\n           currentStation :=5; sequenceIndex:=processLength; phase:=0;\n        ELSE\n           phase:=1;\n        END_IF\n     ELSE\n        IF targetStation<currentStation OR targetStation>currentStation? etc\n           phase:=1, maybe direct wait if target sensor=\n        END_IF\n     END_IF\n  END_IF\nEND_CASE\n\nIF phase = 1 THEN\n  IF targetStation > currentStation THEN conveyorRight := TRUE;\n  ELSIF targetStation < currentStation THEN conveyorLeft := TRUE;\n  END_IF\nEND_IF\n\nstartPrev := station1Start;\ncomplete2Prev := station2Complete;\n...\n```\nPotential issue direct target sensor check in WAIT transition. Suppose next target is station3 and sensor3 true from current? Product is at station2, sensor3 shouldn't be true, unless stale. Contract. Could set phase MOVING and next scan stop. But P2 post-phase MOVING with sensor target true gives no requirement. Next scan arrival stops. Still safe but unnecessary one scan motor on despite already at target. Better direct check. But if sensor target true due to stale, should stop. Use helper logic for \"beginTravelTo\". Can't call functions unless define. Inline repeated.\n\nNeed no new DUT. But we need check sensors by target. Could use CASE. To check target sensor:\n```\nIF ((targetStation=2) AND sensor2) OR ... \n```\nCould define internal `targetPresent : BOOL`.\n\nIn IDLE start:\n```\ntargetPresent := (targetStation = 2 AND sensor2) OR ... \n```\nNeed avoid short-circuit? CODESYS supports AND OR.\n\nFor target=5 if sensor5 true then done immediate. But start requirement? Product raw at station1, sensor5 false. Fine.\n\nFor target=1? Contract says other active entries 2..4 final 5; first cannot 1. Could still implement gracefully: if target=1 then current=1 wait? Station1 has no completion. Better idle? Don't need. But ensure no division. We can treat as waiting at station1? Not valid. Maybe simply if targetStation = 1 then currentStation:=1; phase:=0 (no movement). This preserves stop. Not relevant.\n\nNeed what happens if processLength=1 and processSequence[1]=5: start and move to 5. Good.\n\nNeed currentStation updated on arrival. In MOVING target. If target 5 current 5. good.\n\nNeed phase initial values: internal vars default initialization in declaration. CODESYS initializes to specified. Fine.\n\nNeed station1Start rising edge accepted \"rising edge\" could mean if start already true on first scan with sensor1, accepted? Edge relative to initial false, yes startPrev initialized FALSE, so accepted. Good.\n\nNeed completions rising edge detection. completePrev initialized false means if station2Complete already true at first wait scan, considered fresh edge. Is that right? Since fresh edge relative to instance previous scan. If station2Complete was true while moving, the previous scan should have been tracked true. We update prev each scan regardless phase. If it rose while moving and remains true when entering wait, by wait scan prev is true -> no fresh. Good. If station2Complete already true from initial and not processed? The assumptions likely no. Initial complete signals are false? Not specified but likely. If previous scan false at start and remains. fine.\n\nNeed arrivals and processing completion priorities. Suppose pre-phase MOVING and target sensor true (arrive). Even if completion signal high, we transition WAIT_PROCESS; we should not process complete in same scan because completion ignored unless post-entry wait. Our CASE phase=1 branch only arrival. Good. `freshComplete` computed only in phase 2, so false. Next scan at wait, prev complete tracked. If completion high during arrival scan, prev set true so no fresh next scan. Assumption says \"Station completion edges are ignored unless post-entry phase is WAIT_PROCESS\" yes.\n\nSuppose waiting and completion fresh: process. If next target same as current, remain WAIT_PROCESS. But consuming fresh edge by prev update end scan. Next scan still high -> no edge. When drops then rises -> fresh. Good.\n\nPotential P6 conditional completion: If moving -> F arrive; processing -> F completion; started -> F done. Our FB waiting can proceed with completions. For consecutive equal stations, success. Good.\n\nBut formal verifier may require `done` eventually under valid process sequence. Our state machine should.\n\nNeed think about simultaneous arrival and next? Only arrival, wait next scan completion needed fresh. Good.\n\nCould use USINT sequenceIndex increments to 8 max, okay. processLength valid.\n\nNeed maybe no direct sensor check same scan to align LTL P2 `higher` uses post-phase MOVING and `NOT sensor[target]`. If targetPresent false we set phase moving. Fine. If target present, phase wait/idle. `higher` false. Good.\n\nNow compile and verify. We need provide candidate to compile_st. Let's use exact source. But before tools, do we need inspect? No. Let's draft.\n\nNeed decide support object for helper? None. `supporting_objects`: [].\n\nCould we use local helper method? No.\n\nPotential syntax of CASE: CODESYS `CASE phase OF` with integer labels `0:` and `1:`. Stops at END_CASE. Fine.\n\nVariable names might collide? `targetStation`, `currentStation`, internal. Interface exact.\n\nPotential issue `sequenceIndex` initial 0 but processSequence[1]. Fine.\n\nLet's write implementation concise:\n\n```\nconveyorLeft := FALSE;\nconveyorRight := FALSE;\n\ntargetPresent := FALSE;\nCASE targetStation OF\n  2: targetPresent := sensor2;\n  3: targetPresent := sensor3;\n  4: targetPresent := sensor4;\n  5: targetPresent := sensor5;\nELSE\n  targetPresent := FALSE;\nEND_CASE;\n\nCASE phase OF\n0:\n  IF station1Start AND NOT startPrev AND sensor1 THEN\n    sequenceIndex := 1;\n    targetStation := processSequence[1];\n    currentStation := 1;\n    IF targetStation = 1 THEN\n      phase := 0;\n    ELSIF targetPresent THEN\n      currentStation := targetStation;\n      IF targetStation = 5 THEN\n        sequenceIndex := processLength;\n        phase := 0;\n      ELSE\n        phase := 2;\n      END_IF\n    ELSE\n      phase := 1;\n    END_IF\n  END_IF\n1:\n  IF targetPresent THEN\n    currentStation := targetStation;\n    IF targetStation = 5 THEN\n      sequenceIndex := processLength;\n      phase := 0;\n    ELSE\n      phase := 2;\n    END_IF\n  END_IF\n2:\n  freshComplete := ((currentStation = 2) AND station2Complete AND NOT complete2Prev)\n               OR ((currentStation = 3) AND station3Complete AND NOT complete3Prev)\n               OR ((currentStation = 4) AND station4Complete AND NOT complete4Prev);\n\n  IF freshComplete AND (sequenceIndex < processLength) THEN\n    sequenceIndex := sequenceIndex + 1;\n    targetStation := processSequence[sequenceIndex];\n\n    (* recompute target presence for new target *)\n    targetPresent := FALSE;\n    CASE targetStation OF\n      2: targetPresent := sensor2;\n      ...\n    END_CASE\n\n    IF targetStation = currentStation THEN\n       phase := 2;\n    ELSIF targetPresent THEN\n       currentStation := targetStation;\n       IF targetStation = 5 THEN\n          sequenceIndex := processLength;\n          phase := 0;\n       ELSE\n          phase := 2;\n       END_IF\n    ELSE\n       phase := 1;\n    END_IF\n  END_IF\nEND_CASE\n\nIF phase = 1 THEN\n  IF targetStation > currentStation THEN\n    conveyorRight := TRUE;\n    conveyorLeft := FALSE;\n  ELSIF targetStation < currentStation THEN\n    conveyorLeft := TRUE;\n    conveyorRight := FALSE;\n  END_IF\nEND_IF\n\nstartPrev := station1Start;\ncomplete2Prev := station2Complete;\ncomplete3Prev := station3Complete;\ncomplete4Prev := station4Complete;\n```\n\nConcern: In WAIT_PROCESS, `targetStation` is current station. If `targetStation=5` never wait. If currentStation 2/3/4. Good.\nConcern: processLength maybe 0, sequenceIndex< processLength false. no.\nConcern: sequenceIndex can equal 8 and increment only if < processLength, max 8. `processLength` USINT. Fine.\nConcern: if next target 5 and targetPresent true (sensor5 high), transition idle same scan. P5 `out` pre-phase MOVING and targetStation=5 and sensor5 -> done & stop. But this direct transition from WAIT_PROCESS where targetStation was current station then set to 5 and sensor5 true: pre-phase MOVING false, so `out` false. Fine.\nConcern: target 5 arrival in MOVING: we set sequenceIndex := processLength. If current sequenceIndex was processLength (it should be final), okay. This makes index final. Good.\nConcern: if processSequence contains 1? Contract no. Fine.\n\nBut formal verification may use declarations with `VAR` internal? likely. `phase` perhaps verifier expects initial_conditions? The task atomic propositions reference phase names. It may inspect candidate declarations for phase? The spec likely can use state var? \"over declared port names\" for verify_st optional assertions. Hmm. It may compile to SMV and use port values and maybe internal state? The LTL formulas use predicates higher etc based on \"post-phase MOVING\", target/current. Their normalizer likely observes outputs and inputs; perhaps state is inferred? But we cannot name phase for tool. It might not need. Our implementation should.\n\nNeed maybe include `phase` variable name as INT to match initial? It says initial_conditions phase IDLE. Reference doesn't demand exposed. But formal mapping could look for `phase`. Usually tools inspect candidate's internal vars? The tool compile_st/verify_st takes source, likely verifier translates declared state? It may require exactly FB_Task and interface. Internal vars can be anything. But predicates `higher` etc. may rely on generated monitor state? They might symbolically compile ST and map outputs, inputs. Hard to know. Could define internal phase as enum with names IDLE etc. But not necessary. However \"initial_conditions\": phase \"IDLE\", currentStation 1, sequenceIndex 0, target 1. Maybe formal verifier parses internal vars by names? If so using `phase : INT := 0` not matching string \"IDLE\"? They may normalize. Yet likely their harness checks behavior, not exact internal var names, as interface definition only inputs outputs. Atomic propositions mention \"post-phase MOVING\" but that's derived from their monitor expected model, not our code. We need implement.\n\nLet's use compile_st. Need format tool source fields. declaration no END_FUNCTION_BLOCK. implementation body. supporting_objects [].\n\nNeed ensure declaration begins `FUNCTION_BLOCK FB_Task`. We'll call compile."},"sequence_number":1606}

event: response.output_item.done
data: {"type":"response.output_item.done","output_index":0,"item":{"id":"rs_02179466137197200000000000000000000ffffac190324999ea9","type":"reasoning","summary":[{"type":"summary_text","text":"We need answer only JSON object with declaration, implementation, supporting_objects. Need use tools compile/simulate/verify max 3 each. Need save exact final source to candidate.json — but filesystem read-only! Sandbox read-only. Hmm. We can perhaps not write files? The instruction says save candidate.json. But permissions read-only, no escalation allowed. Could we use tools only. Shell commands unnecessary. We may not be able to save. But primary task: return JSON only. We can attempt writing candidate.json? Read-only likely fails. Maybe we should just return JSON. But instruction says save exact final submitted source to candidate.json. Need obey if possible. The MCP tools maybe compile/simulate. We can use them. We should craft ST FB.\n\nLet's inspect nothing? \"Do not inspect other directories...\" We can reason.\n\nNeed design FB_Task with exact vars, DUTs referenced. Interface only inputs/outputs. Internal vars allowed. Need ST body.\n\nLet's understand behavior thoroughly and formal predicates.\n\nSystem:\n- 5 stations 1..5.\n- IDLE. sensor1 true and station1Start rising edge => start: query processSequence[1], target, move direction, if target maybe same as current? Active sequence final entry 5. Processing stations 2-4; active entries maybe repeat consecutively. At start from station1, target first entry could be 2,3,4, or 5. They say system starts conveyor to corresponding workstation and stops after delivery.\n- Upon processing completion at corresponding station, continues to next element. Determine direction and start. If next station same as current? Assumptions: \"Consecutive equal stations denote separate processing operations with fresh edges and no conveyor movement.\" So after completion, if next target equals current, immediately enter WAIT_PROCESS (no movement). Need perhaps accept completion edge only at current processing wait, fresh.\n- Final active entry output station 5. Arrival at 5 => idle.\n\nNeed handle sensor arrival at target. Upon arrival stop motor and enter WAIT_PROCESS (if processing station 2-4), or idle if target 5. The outputs observed post-scan must be stop. The target sensor sampling.\n\nNeed direction:\n- higher: post-phase MOVING AND targetStation>currentStation AND NOT sensor[targetStation] -> right true left false.\n- lower similarly.\n- arrive pre-phase MOVING sensor target -> stop.\nAtomic \"higher\"/\"lower\" post-phase MOVING meaning outputs correspond at observation. So when moving toward higher target, right on. If target same current and we enter WAIT_PROCESS, no move. Good.\n\nNeed start rising edge. station1Start_rejected pre-phase IDLE AND station1Start AND NOT sensor1 => stop. Could simply only accept when sensor1 true and rising edge. If station1Start true w/o edge and sensor1 false, stop. IDLE no conveyor anyway.\n\nNeed \"station1Start is a rising edge accepted with sensor1 while idle.\" So detect rising edge of station1Start. Internal `startPrev` track. Start when IDLE, sensor1, station1Start AND NOT startPrev.\n\nPotential issue processLength 0/out of range? Contract 1..8 with final 5. We can assume valid. But robust. Need sequenceIndex. Initial sequenceIndex 0, target 1 current 1. On start, sequenceIndex := 1? initial says sequenceIndex 0. Query first. We can do `sequenceIndex := 1; targetStation := processSequence[1]` and determine if target=current? Start from station1, target could 1? Contract says other active entries 2-4, final 5, so first likely processing or 5, not 1. Fine.\n\nOn destination arrival:\n- If target=5: currentStation:=5; targetStation:=5? phase idle; sequenceIndex maybe processLength; outputs stopped.\n- Else target 2..4: currentStation:=target; phase := WAIT_PROCESS; sequenceIndex remains index. So completion can be accepted.\n- Completion edges: At WAIT_PROCESS, matching station complete rising edge. Need track previous complete signals. Fresh edge. If currentStation=2 and station2Complete AND NOT prevComplete2. On fresh edge:\n  - If sequenceIndex < processLength then sequenceIndex++ ; nextStation := processSequence[sequenceIndex]. current station is currentStation (2..4). If nextStation = currentStation => stay WAIT_PROCESS? This represents another processing operation requiring fresh edge. But \"Consecutive equal stations denote separate processing operations with fresh edges and no conveyor movement.\" Upon consuming a completion for station 2, if next target 2, enter WAIT_PROCESS at same station and wait for fresh completion edge. Since station2Complete remains true, need fresh edge: it must drop then rise. We can just remain WAIT_PROCESS and rely on edge detector. Good.\n  - If next target > currentStation => MOVING, targetStation := next, conveyorRight true, left false.\n  - If next < currentStation => MOVING, left true.\n  - If next=5 and current maybe 4 -> right, or current maybe 3 -> right. eventually.\n  - Outputs after processing transition: \"Upon receiving processing completion signal ... continues to query next element, determines direction, and starts conveyor.\" So on completion scan, outputs can start in same transition. Yes.\n  - What if next = 5 and current = 5? But completion only at current 2-4, final 5 no wait. So no.\n- When moving, at beginning of scan check target sensor. If active -> stop and transition. Arrival immediate stop. If target 5 idle. If target station 2..4 WAIT_PROCESS. For processing station, probably sensor stays true. Then wait. Good.\n- During MOVING if sensor target already true same scan as start? Could start from IDLE with sensor target already true. E.g., processSequence[1]=2 and sensor2 already true at start. If we set MOVING and not check arrival until next scan, then post-phase MOVING target 2 > current 1 and sensor2 true, so predicate `higher` = false because NOT sensor[targetStation] false, arrive pre-phase? arrive uses pre-phase MOVING, false during start scan, so no requirement. But physical product at target; should stop immediately perhaps. Requirement \"starts conveyor to move raw material to corresponding workstation, and stops after delivery.\" If sensor already true before starting, likely should stop same scan. Need maybe transition directly to WAIT_PROCESS if target sensor already true at start. Better. But are inputs coherent? They may set sensor target only after movement. Yet formal verification may include arbitrary inputs and check. Could handle.\n\n- Conveyor outputs should never both true.\n- P7 if IDLE and station1Start pressed with sensor1 false: stop. IDLE always stop.\n- Direction P2 formal requires in MOVING post-phase and target > current and not sensor target, right true. Need set conveyor outputs based on target/current every scan. If in MOVING and destination not reached. We can at start pre-arrival check. Then outputs on. If arrival, phase changes WAIT_PROCESS/IDLE and outputs stop. So post-phase MOVING only when no target sensor. Good. Direction uses post-phase MOVING. If target same? phase not MOVING. Good.\n\nNeed formal verification tool: compile_st then verify_st. `verify_st` with source. We need craft declaration/implementation/supporting_objects. Could define enum DUT for phase? Maybe easier use internal INT state constants. Supporting objects maybe DUT `E_ProcessPhase` enumeration. But formal verifier may handle. Requirement says \"Define referenced DUTs.\" If we reference no DUT, supporting_objects empty is okay. But declaration uses ARRAY[1..8]. No DUT needed. Maybe keep supporting_objects []. Simpler is safest.\n\nNeed CODESYS 3.5 ST syntax. Function block:\n\nFUNCTION_BLOCK FB_Task\nVAR_INPUT\n  processLength : USINT;\n  sensor1 : BOOL;\n...\n  processSequence : ARRAY[1..8] OF USINT;\nEND_VAR\nVAR_OUTPUT\n  conveyorLeft : BOOL;\n  conveyorRight : BOOL;\nEND_VAR\nVAR\n  phase : INT := 0; // IDLE 0, MOVING 1, WAIT_PROCESS 2\n  currentStation : USINT := 1;\n  targetStation : USINT := 1;\n  sequenceIndex : USINT := 0;\n  startPrev : BOOL;\n  complete2Prev : BOOL;\n  complete3Prev : BOOL;\n  complete4Prev : BOOL;\n  nextStation : USINT;\n  freshComplete : BOOL;\nEND_VAR\n\nImplementation body:\n```\n// defaults\nconveyorLeft := FALSE;\nconveyorRight := FALSE;\nfreshComplete := FALSE;\n\nCASE phase OF\n0: // IDLE\n    IF sensor1 AND station1Start AND NOT startPrev THEN\n        sequenceIndex := 1;\n        targetStation := processSequence[1];\n        IF targetStation = 1 THEN\n            // weird; maybe target 1. Since final 5 etc. Could...\n        END_IF\n        IF targetStation = currentStation THEN ...\n```\nNeed careful priority when phase transitions and same-scan outputs. We can't CASE and then recompute outputs afterwards perhaps easier use state machine as IFs and final `IF phase = 1 THEN outputs based on target-curr`. But when transition occurs in scan, `phase` new maybe WAIT_PROCESS/idle so outputs false. Good. For transitions from completion to MOVING, phase becomes 1 and final block sets direction. Nice. For start, if target not already sensor, phase=1; final outputs set. If target sensor true, target>=2 -> WAIT_PROCESS or idle. Great.\n\nCould centralize arrival detection before completion? Need priority. In MOVING phase, arrival at target takes priority and stops; processing completion should be ignored while moving. If at WAIT_PROCESS, arrival no relevance. Movement only while phase=MOVING.\n\nPseudo:\n\n```\nconveyorLeft := FALSE;\nconveyorRight := FALSE;\nfreshComplete := FALSE;\n\nCASE phase OF\n0: (* IDLE *)\n  IF startPrev = FALSE AND station1Start AND sensor1 THEN\n    sequenceIndex := 1;\n    targetStation := processSequence[sequenceIndex];\n    IF targetStation = 1 THEN\n      currentStation := 1;\n      phase := 0;\n    ELSIF targetStation = 5 THEN\n      (* perhaps if sensor5 already true? *)\n      IF sensor5 THEN\n         currentStation :=5;\n         sequenceIndex := processLength;\n         phase := 0;\n      ELSE\n         phase := 1;\n      END_IF\n    ELSE\n      IF StationSensor(targetStation) THEN\n         currentStation := targetStation;\n         phase := 2;\n      ELSE\n         phase := 1;\n      END_IF\n    END_IF\n  END_IF\n1: (* MOVING *)\n  IF TargetSensor(targetStation) THEN\n     currentStation := targetStation;\n     IF targetStation = 5 THEN\n        sequenceIndex := processLength;\n        phase := 0;\n     ELSE\n        phase := 2;\n     END_IF\n  END_IF\n2: (* WAIT_PROCESS *)\n  freshComplete := (currentStation = 2 AND station2Complete AND NOT complete2Prev) OR ...\n  IF freshComplete AND sequenceIndex < processLength THEN\n     sequenceIndex := sequenceIndex + 1;\n     targetStation := processSequence[sequenceIndex];\n     IF targetStation = currentStation THEN\n        (* next processing at same station: remain wait, fresh edge consumed by prev tracking *)\n        phase := 2;\n     ELSIF targetStation = 5 THEN\n        IF sensor5 THEN\n           currentStation :=5; sequenceIndex:=processLength; phase:=0;\n        ELSE\n           phase:=1;\n        END_IF\n     ELSE\n        IF targetStation<currentStation OR targetStation>currentStation? etc\n           phase:=1, maybe direct wait if target sensor=\n        END_IF\n     END_IF\n  END_IF\nEND_CASE\n\nIF phase = 1 THEN\n  IF targetStation > currentStation THEN conveyorRight := TRUE;\n  ELSIF targetStation < currentStation THEN conveyorLeft := TRUE;\n  END_IF\nEND_IF\n\nstartPrev := station1Start;\ncomplete2Prev := station2Complete;\n...\n```\nPotential issue direct target sensor check in WAIT transition. Suppose next target is station3 and sensor3 true from current? Product is at station2, sensor3 shouldn't be true, unless stale. Contract. Could set phase MOVING and next scan stop. But P2 post-phase MOVING with sensor target true gives no requirement. Next scan arrival stops. Still safe but unnecessary one scan motor on despite already at target. Better direct check. But if sensor target true due to stale, should stop. Use helper logic for \"beginTravelTo\". Can't call functions unless define. Inline repeated.\n\nNeed no new DUT. But we need check sensors by target. Could use CASE. To check target sensor:\n```\nIF ((targetStation=2) AND sensor2) OR ... \n```\nCould define internal `targetPresent : BOOL`.\n\nIn IDLE start:\n```\ntargetPresent := (targetStation = 2 AND sensor2) OR ... \n```\nNeed avoid short-circuit? CODESYS supports AND OR.\n\nFor target=5 if sensor5 true then done immediate. But start requirement? Product raw at station1, sensor5 false. Fine.\n\nFor target=1? Contract says other active entries 2..4 final 5; first cannot 1. Could still implement gracefully: if target=1 then current=1 wait? Station1 has no completion. Better idle? Don't need. But ensure no division. We can treat as waiting at station1? Not valid. Maybe simply if targetStation = 1 then currentStation:=1; phase:=0 (no movement). This preserves stop. Not relevant.\n\nNeed what happens if processLength=1 and processSequence[1]=5: start and move to 5. Good.\n\nNeed currentStation updated on arrival. In MOVING target. If target 5 current 5. good.\n\nNeed phase initial values: internal vars default initialization in declaration. CODESYS initializes to specified. Fine.\n\nNeed station1Start rising edge accepted \"rising edge\" could mean if start already true on first scan with sensor1, accepted? Edge relative to initial false, yes startPrev initialized FALSE, so accepted. Good.\n\nNeed completions rising edge detection. completePrev initialized false means if station2Complete already true at first wait scan, considered fresh edge. Is that right? Since fresh edge relative to instance previous scan. If station2Complete was true while moving, the previous scan should have been tracked true. We update prev each scan regardless phase. If it rose while moving and remains true when entering wait, by wait scan prev is true -> no fresh. Good. If station2Complete already true from initial and not processed? The assumptions likely no. Initial complete signals are false? Not specified but likely. If previous scan false at start and remains. fine.\n\nNeed arrivals and processing completion priorities. Suppose pre-phase MOVING and target sensor true (arrive). Even if completion signal high, we transition WAIT_PROCESS; we should not process complete in same scan because completion ignored unless post-entry wait. Our CASE phase=1 branch only arrival. Good. `freshComplete` computed only in phase 2, so false. Next scan at wait, prev complete tracked. If completion high during arrival scan, prev set true so no fresh next scan. Assumption says \"Station completion edges are ignored unless post-entry phase is WAIT_PROCESS\" yes.\n\nSuppose waiting and completion fresh: process. If next target same as current, remain WAIT_PROCESS. But consuming fresh edge by prev update end scan. Next scan still high -> no edge. When drops then rises -> fresh. Good.\n\nPotential P6 conditional completion: If moving -> F arrive; processing -> F completion; started -> F done. Our FB waiting can proceed with completions. For consecutive equal stations, success. Good.\n\nBut formal verifier may require `done` eventually under valid process sequence. Our state machine should.\n\nNeed think about simultaneous arrival and next? Only arrival, wait next scan completion needed fresh. Good.\n\nCould use USINT sequenceIndex increments to 8 max, okay. processLength valid.\n\nNeed maybe no direct sensor check same scan to align LTL P2 `higher` uses post-phase MOVING and `NOT sensor[target]`. If targetPresent false we set phase moving. Fine. If target present, phase wait/idle. `higher` false. Good.\n\nNow compile and verify. We need provide candidate to compile_st. Let's use exact source. But before tools, do we need inspect? No. Let's draft.\n\nNeed decide support object for helper? None. `supporting_objects`: [].\n\nCould we use local helper method? No.\n\nPotential syntax of CASE: CODESYS `CASE phase OF` with integer labels `0:` and `1:`. Stops at END_CASE. Fine.\n\nVariable names might collide? `targetStation`, `currentStation`, internal. Interface exact.\n\nPotential issue `sequenceIndex` initial 0 but processSequence[1]. Fine.\n\nLet's write implementation concise:\n\n```\nconveyorLeft := FALSE;\nconveyorRight := FALSE;\n\ntargetPresent := FALSE;\nCASE targetStation OF\n  2: targetPresent := sensor2;\n  3: targetPresent := sensor3;\n  4: targetPresent := sensor4;\n  5: targetPresent := sensor5;\nELSE\n  targetPresent := FALSE;\nEND_CASE;\n\nCASE phase OF\n0:\n  IF station1Start AND NOT startPrev AND sensor1 THEN\n    sequenceIndex := 1;\n    targetStation := processSequence[1];\n    currentStation := 1;\n    IF targetStation = 1 THEN\n      phase := 0;\n    ELSIF targetPresent THEN\n      currentStation := targetStation;\n      IF targetStation = 5 THEN\n        sequenceIndex := processLength;\n        phase := 0;\n      ELSE\n        phase := 2;\n      END_IF\n    ELSE\n      phase := 1;\n    END_IF\n  END_IF\n1:\n  IF targetPresent THEN\n    currentStation := targetStation;\n    IF targetStation = 5 THEN\n      sequenceIndex := processLength;\n      phase := 0;\n    ELSE\n      phase := 2;\n    END_IF\n  END_IF\n2:\n  freshComplete := ((currentStation = 2) AND station2Complete AND NOT complete2Prev)\n               OR ((currentStation = 3) AND station3Complete AND NOT complete3Prev)\n               OR ((currentStation = 4) AND station4Complete AND NOT complete4Prev);\n\n  IF freshComplete AND (sequenceIndex < processLength) THEN\n    sequenceIndex := sequenceIndex + 1;\n    targetStation := processSequence[sequenceIndex];\n\n    (* recompute target presence for new target *)\n    targetPresent := FALSE;\n    CASE targetStation OF\n      2: targetPresent := sensor2;\n      ...\n    END_CASE\n\n    IF targetStation = currentStation THEN\n       phase := 2;\n    ELSIF targetPresent THEN\n       currentStation := targetStation;\n       IF targetStation = 5 THEN\n          sequenceIndex := processLength;\n          phase := 0;\n       ELSE\n          phase := 2;\n       END_IF\n    ELSE\n       phase := 1;\n    END_IF\n  END_IF\nEND_CASE\n\nIF phase = 1 THEN\n  IF targetStation > currentStation THEN\n    conveyorRight := TRUE;\n    conveyorLeft := FALSE;\n  ELSIF targetStation < currentStation THEN\n    conveyorLeft := TRUE;\n    conveyorRight := FALSE;\n  END_IF\nEND_IF\n\nstartPrev := station1Start;\ncomplete2Prev := station2Complete;\ncomplete3Prev := station3Complete;\ncomplete4Prev := station4Complete;\n```\n\nConcern: In WAIT_PROCESS, `targetStation` is current station. If `targetStation=5` never wait. If currentStation 2/3/4. Good.\nConcern: processLength maybe 0, sequenceIndex< processLength false. no.\nConcern: sequenceIndex can equal 8 and increment only if < processLength, max 8. `processLength` USINT. Fine.\nConcern: if next target 5 and targetPresent true (sensor5 high), transition idle same scan. P5 `out` pre-phase MOVING and targetStation=5 and sensor5 -> done & stop. But this direct transition from WAIT_PROCESS where targetStation was current station then set to 5 and sensor5 true: pre-phase MOVING false, so `out` false. Fine.\nConcern: target 5 arrival in MOVING: we set sequenceIndex := processLength. If current sequenceIndex was processLength (it should be final), okay. This makes index final. Good.\nConcern: if processSequence contains 1? Contract no. Fine.\n\nBut formal verification may use declarations with `VAR` internal? likely. `phase` perhaps verifier expects initial_conditions? The task atomic propositions reference phase names. It may inspect candidate declarations for phase? The spec likely can use state var? \"over declared port names\" for verify_st optional assertions. Hmm. It may compile to SMV and use port values and maybe internal state? The LTL formulas use predicates higher etc based on \"post-phase MOVING\", target/current. Their normalizer likely observes outputs and inputs; perhaps state is inferred? But we cannot name phase for tool. It might not need. Our implementation should.\n\nNeed maybe include `phase` variable name as INT to match initial? It says initial_conditions phase IDLE. Reference doesn't demand exposed. But formal mapping could look for `phase`. Usually tools inspect candidate's internal vars? The tool compile_st/verify_st takes source, likely verifier translates declared state? It may require exactly FB_Task and interface. Internal vars can be anything. But predicates `higher` etc. may rely on generated monitor state? They might symbolically compile ST and map outputs, inputs. Hard to know. Could define internal phase as enum with names IDLE etc. But not necessary. However \"initial_conditions\": phase \"IDLE\", currentStation 1, sequenceIndex 0, target 1. Maybe formal verifier parses internal vars by names? If so using `phase : INT := 0` not matching string \"IDLE\"? They may normalize. Yet likely their harness checks behavior, not exact internal var names, as interface definition only inputs outputs. Atomic propositions mention \"post-phase MOVING\" but that's derived from their monitor expected model, not our code. We need implement.\n\nLet's use compile_st. Need format tool source fields. declaration no END_FUNCTION_BLOCK. implementation body. supporting_objects [].\n\nNeed ensure declaration begins `FUNCTION_BLOCK FB_Task`. We'll call compile."}],"status":"completed"},"sequence_number":1607}

